# Normalise line endings in the repository itself, not per-machine.
#
# Without this, `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows checked every text file out as
# CRLF while the index held LF, so `git status` reported ~100 files as modified
# when not one byte of content had changed. That is not cosmetic: agents read
# `git status` to decide what they own, and a status that lies about 100 files
# trains everyone to ignore it.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text vs binary. `eol=lf` pins the working tree to
# LF on every platform, including this one, so what is committed is what is
# read. Windows tools that require CRLF (PowerShell, `.ps1`, `.iss`) are pinned
# explicitly below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell and CI scripts: the shell wants LF even on Windows.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf

# Windows installer scripts are read by Inno Setup, which is happier with CRLF.
*.iss text eol=crlf

# Binary assets. Never touched by the text filter.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.onnx binary
*.f32 binary
*.wav binary
*.mp4 binary

# Golden fixtures are data, not text. Their digests are checked by
# `vaani-qa`'s golden guard, and a CRLF round-trip must never change one.
tests/fixtures/** -text
